.TH ETHERUSB 1
.SH NAME
etherusb \- serve a USB ethernet device as a netif file tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B usb/etherusb
[
.B -d
]
[
.B -m
.I mountpoint
]
[
.B -a
.I macaddress
]
.I device
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Etherusb
is a Limbo driver in the shape of 9front's
.BR nusb/ether .
It serves the
.IR netif (3)
file interface that
.IR ip (3)'s
.B ethermedium
binds to, backed by the bulk endpoints of a USB ethernet device reached
through
.BR #U .
.PP
The point of serving it rather than writing a kernel card is that
.B ethermedium
reaches an ethernet device purely by path \(em it opens
.IR clone ,
.IR addr
and
.IR N/data
under whatever directory it is given \(em so this Styx server
.I is
devip's ethernet, and gets ARP, IPv6 neighbour discovery and
multicast-MAC mapping from the kernel for free.  A kernel
.IR ether (3)
card cannot take this role at all: cards are probed at boot, before
.IR usbd (4)
has enumerated any USB device.
.PP
The options are
.TP
.B -d
Report what the driver is doing on standard error.
.TP
.BI -m " mountpoint"
Where to serve the tree; the default is
.BR /net.alt ,
so that it does not displace a kernel
.B /net
until something binds it there.
.TP
.BI -a " macaddress"
Use this MAC address rather than the one the device reports.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/usb/etherusb.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR usbd (4),
.IR ip (3),
.IR netif (3)
.SH BUGS
One device per invocation.
